Stage 3 — Practical Java multithreading
16) thread pool (why executors exist)

Creating a thread per task doesn’t scale (slow, memory heavy).

ExecutorService manages a pool of reusable threads + a queue of tasks.

You submit tasks (Runnable/Callable), the pool assigns them to worker threads.

ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
pool.execute(() -> doTask());
pool.shutdown();

17) configuring a thread pool (deep dive)

Under the hood, the core implementation is ThreadPoolExecutor:

ExecutorService pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
    4,                       // corePoolSize: kept alive even if idle
    8,                       // maximumPoolSize: cap when queue is full
    60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,   // keepAliveTime for extra threads
    new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(100), // work queue (bounded!)
    Executors.defaultThreadFactory(), // naming, daemon, priority
    new ThreadPoolExecutor.AbortPolicy() // rejection when saturated
);

Choosing values (rules of thumb)

Queue:

Unbounded (LinkedBlockingQueue) → simple, but risks OOM if producers are faster than consumers. Often used with fixed pool.

Bounded (ArrayBlockingQueue) → back-pressure; choose a size based on throughput/memory.

SynchronousQueue → no queue; handoff tasks directly to threads; use with cached pools.

Pool sizes:

CPU-bound tasks: corePoolSize ≈ number of cores (e.g., Runtime.getRuntime().availableProcessors()).

IO-bound tasks: you can go higher because threads wait on IO (e.g., ~ cores × 2..4, measure).

Rejection policies:

AbortPolicy (default): throws RejectedExecutionException.

CallerRunsPolicy: submitter runs the task (slows producers = back-pressure).

DiscardPolicy / DiscardOldestPolicy: drop tasks (only if you can lose work).

ThreadFactory:

Name threads for debugging; decide daemon vs non-daemon.

ThreadFactory named = r -> {
    Thread t = new Thread(r);
    t.setName("worker-" + t.getId());
    t.setDaemon(false);
    return t;
};


Shutdown: always call shutdown() (then awaitTermination) or shutdownNow() for an emergency stop.

Specialized pools

Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n) → fixed size + unbounded queue.

Executors.newCachedThreadPool() → elastic (0..∞), uses SynchronousQueue.

Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor() → single worker, tasks are serialized.

ScheduledThreadPoolExecutor → delayed & periodic tasks (cron-like).

18) how servlets handle multiple requests

Servlet containers (Tomcat/Jetty/Undertow) keep a request thread pool.

One HTTP request = one thread from that pool (simplified model).

Your servlet instance is shared across threads:

Don’t store request data in fields; use local variables only.

If you must keep shared mutable state, make it thread-safe (synchronize, atomics, or concurrent collections).

Async/non-blocking (Servlet 3.0+) lets a request free the container thread while waiting for IO; completion runs later.

Tuning example (Tomcat): maxThreads, minSpareThreads, acceptCount control concurrency/queuing (configured in server, not in your code).
